# Target C-II — Carathéodory sparsifies the live diameter-resultant interface

**Date:** 2026-09-15<br>
**Status:** proof-only source-level structural strengthening<br>
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844`, on the audited high-phase smallest-leaf O--O path<br>
**Dependencies:** exact laminar interval support for actual diameter rows; positive normalized diameter KKT multipliers; audited Section 157 cut resultants; audited high-phase adjacent-cut path and phase-defect budget; adjacent switching-layer/caterpillar theorem<br>
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_caratheodory_sparse_resultant_profile.py`<br>
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The current adjacent-cut normal form still carries as many as `1800+o(1)` actual
switching diameter edges.  That is already finite-scale, but it is too large for a
direct geometric classification.

There is a second, independent sparsification available at the level of the exact KKT
**cut resultants**.  Concatenate all selected-cut resultant equations and apply
Carathéodory to the actual diameter rows.  The resulting nonnegative diameter measure
preserves every selected cut resultant exactly and uses at most `2m+1` actual diameter
rows on an `m`-cut path.  Since each diameter row has interval support, the sparse
measure has at most `4m+2` switch events in total.

Combining that global switch count with the already-audited bounded total phase defect
forces an adjacent interface at which the exact resultant jump is carried by only a
handful of actual diameter vectors.

The strongest small-support corollary obtained below is:

\[
\boxed{
\begin{gathered}
\text{there is an adjacent leaf-cut pair }(i,i+1)\text{ and a nonnegative}\
\text{actual-diameter resultant certificate preserving every path cut exactly,}\
\text{for which at most four supported diameter rows switch at }i,\\
B_i^{\rm sp}+d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})
<{97500\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
\end{gathered}}
\tag{CSR.1}
\]

Here `B_i^sp` is the total sparse certificate weight on rows switching at the chosen
boundary.  Consequently

\[
\boxed{
F_{i+1}-F_i
=\sum_{h\in J_i}\widetilde\beta_h
 (\varepsilon_{h,i+1}-\varepsilon_{hi})r_h,
\qquad |J_i|\le4,
}
\tag{CSR.2}
\]

with

\[
\sum_{h\in J_i}\widetilde\beta_h
<{97500\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
\tag{CSR.3}
\]

Thus the change between two asymptotically parallel train resultants is represented by
at most four **actual diameter vectors**.  Those rows lie in the adjacent switching
layer cut and hence inherit its straight-line-thrackle/caterpillar-forest geometry.

This does **not** say that only four actual diameter constraints switch.  It says that
all selected-cut KKT resultant equations admit an exact nonnegative certificate whose
switch at one useful adjacent boundary has support at most four.  The distinction is
load-bearing.

---

## 1. The audited high-phase path

Use the high-phase smallest-leaf path from the triple-small adjacent-cut theorem:

\[
P=(\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_m),
\qquad
m>pD-o(D),
\tag{CSR.4}
\]

where

\[
p={278778993\over78216868750}
=0.0035641799\ldots .
\tag{CSR.5}
\]

Choose coherent nested shores along the path and write

\[
\varepsilon_{hj}\in\{-1,0,1\}
\]

for the signed incidence of actual diameter row `h` on cut `tau_j`.  The laminar
interval theorem gives

\[
(\varepsilon_{h1},\ldots,\varepsilon_{hm})
=0,\ldots,0,s,\ldots,s,0,\ldots,0
\tag{CSR.6}
\]

for one sign `s` depending on `h`.

Let `u_j` be the coherently oriented train contact direction.  The same high-phase path
construction writes

\[
q_j:=\beta_j-\alpha_\gamma-\frac\pi3>0
\]

for its phase offset from the chosen center train and proves

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{j=1}^m q_j
\le Q_{\rm ph}+o(1),
\qquad
Q_{\rm ph}:={C_{\rm phase}\over d_{\rm low}},
}
\tag{CSR.7}
\]

with

\[
d_{\rm low}={9891\over62500}
\]

and the audited outward bound

\[
C_{\rm phase}
<{3258689123681\over612912000000}.
\tag{CSR.8}
\]

In particular the certificate used below has

\[
Q_{\rm ph}<33.595772.
\tag{CSR.9}
\]

For adjacent leaves,

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})
\le q_i+q_{i+1}.
\tag{CSR.10}
\]

---

## 2. Concatenate every selected-cut resultant equation

Let the positive relative-interior diameter multipliers be normalized by

\[
\sum_h\beta_h=1.
\tag{CSR.11}
\]

For each selected cut, Section 157 gives

\[
F_j=A_j u_j
=\sum_h\beta_h\varepsilon_{hj}r_h,
\qquad |r_h|=D,
\tag{CSR.12}
\]

where

\[
A_j=\sum_{e\in E(\tau_j)}\alpha_e>0.
\]

For one actual diameter row define its concatenated cut vector

\[
X_h
:=
(\varepsilon_{h1}r_h,
 \varepsilon_{h2}r_h,
 \ldots,
 \varepsilon_{hm}r_h)
\in\mathbb R^{2m}.
\tag{CSR.13}
\]

Also concatenate the exact cut resultants:

\[
\mathcal F
:=(F_1,F_2,\ldots,F_m)\in\mathbb R^{2m}.
\tag{CSR.14}
\]

Equations (CSR.11)--(CSR.12) say exactly

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal F=\sum_h\beta_h X_h,
\qquad \sum_h\beta_h=1,
\qquad \beta_h>0.
}
\tag{CSR.15}
\]

Thus `mathcal F` lies in the convex hull of the finite set of actual-diameter vectors
`X_h`.

---

## 3. Carathéodory gives an exact `2m+1`-row resultant certificate

Carathéodory's theorem in `R^(2m)` applied to (CSR.15) gives actual diameter rows

\[
h_1,\ldots,h_s,
\qquad s\le2m+1,
\]

and nonnegative coefficients

\[
\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_s,
\qquad
\sum_{a=1}^s\lambda_a=1,
\]

such that

\[
\mathcal F=\sum_{a=1}^s\lambda_a X_{h_a}.
\tag{CSR.16}
\]

Define a sparse diameter measure on the full actual diameter set by

\[
\widetilde\beta_h
:=
\begin{cases}
\lambda_a,&h=h_a,\\
0,&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\tag{CSR.17}
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{
\widetilde\beta_h\ge0,
\qquad
\sum_h\widetilde\beta_h=1,
\qquad
|\operatorname{supp}\widetilde\beta|\le2m+1,
}
\tag{CSR.18}
\]

and, block by block in (CSR.16),

\[
\boxed{
F_j=A_j u_j
=\sum_h\widetilde\beta_h\varepsilon_{hj}r_h
\quad\text{for every }j=1,\ldots,m.
}
\tag{CSR.19}
\]

This is exact, finite, and uses only actual diameter rows of the source.

### Scope warning

The sparse coefficients `tilde beta` are a **cut-resultant certificate**.  They are not
asserted to satisfy the full vertex-by-vertex KKT equilibrium equations.  Carathéodory
here preserves exactly the `2m` selected-cut resultant coordinates and the normalization
in (CSR.18); it does not preserve every other linear statistic of the original KKT
stress.

Accordingly, no unsparsified active diameter constraint may be discarded from the
optimization problem, and no local descent follows from (CSR.19) alone.

---

## 4. The sparse certificate has at most `4m+2` switch events

For an internal path boundary `i=1,...,m-1`, put

\[
\widetilde N_i
:=\#\{h\in\operatorname{supp}\widetilde\beta:
       \varepsilon_{h,i+1}\ne\varepsilon_{hi}\},
\tag{CSR.20}
\]

and

\[
\widetilde B_i
:=\sum_h\widetilde\beta_h
 |\varepsilon_{h,i+1}-\varepsilon_{hi}|.
\tag{CSR.21}
\]

Every interval row changes incidence at at most two internal boundaries.  Hence

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}\widetilde N_i
\le2|\operatorname{supp}\widetilde\beta|
\le4m+2.
}
\tag{CSR.22}
\]

The same interval fact and (CSR.18) give the weighted version

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}\widetilde B_i\le2.
}
\tag{CSR.23}
\]

Because a nonzero interval switch is `0<->s`, its absolute change is exactly one; there
is no hidden factor two in (CSR.21)--(CSR.23).

---

## 5. Many adjacent boundaries have at most `k` sparse switching rows

Fix an integer

\[
k\ge4
\]

and let

\[
G_k:=\{i:\widetilde N_i\le k\}.
\]

Every boundary outside `G_k` spends at least `k+1` switch events.  By (CSR.22),

\[
(m-1-|G_k|)(k+1)\le4m+2.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
|G_k|
\ge
{(k-3)m-(k+3)\over k+1}.
}
\tag{CSR.24}
\]

For fixed `k` and an unbounded survivor sequence, (CSR.4) yields

\[
|G_k|
\ge
\left({k-3\over k+1}p-o(1)\right)D.
\tag{CSR.25}
\]

The special four-row case is already linear:

\[
|G_4|\ge{m-7\over5}.
\tag{CSR.26}
\]

---

## 6. One of those sparse boundaries is also phase-close and low-weight

Restrict the two remaining global budgets to `G_k`.  From (CSR.7),

\[
\sum_{i\in G_k}(q_i+q_{i+1})
\le2Q_{\rm ph}+o(1),
\tag{CSR.27}
\]

while (CSR.23) gives

\[
\sum_{i\in G_k}\widetilde B_i\le2.
\tag{CSR.28}
\]

Thus

\[
\sum_{i\in G_k}
D\bigl(\widetilde B_i+q_i+q_{i+1}\bigr)
\le2D(Q_{\rm ph}+1)+o(D).
\tag{CSR.29}
\]

Using (CSR.25), some `i in G_k` satisfies

\[
\boxed{
D\bigl(\widetilde B_i+q_i+q_{i+1}\bigr)
\le C_k+o(1),
}
\tag{CSR.30}
\]

where

\[
\boxed{
C_k
:=
{2(Q_{\rm ph}+1)(k+1)\over(k-3)p}.
}
\tag{CSR.31}
\]

The exact companion verifier uses only the already-audited rational upper bound
(CSR.8) and proves the following safe Pareto profile:

| maximum sparse switching rows `k` | exact upper estimate for `C_k` | safe rounded constant |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 4 | `97065.16659...` | `97500` |
| 5 | `58239.09996...` | `58500` |
| 6 | `45297.07775...` | `45500` |
| 7 | `38826.06664...` | `39000` |
| 8 | `34943.45998...` | `35000` |

Consequently, for each row of this table there is an adjacent boundary with

\[
\widetilde N_i\le k,
\tag{CSR.32}
\]

\[
\widetilde B_i
<{C_k^{\rm safe}\over D}+o(D^{-1}),
\tag{CSR.33}
\]

and, by (CSR.10),

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})
<{C_k^{\rm safe}\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
\tag{CSR.34}
\]

The two small quantities actually satisfy the stronger combined inequality from
(CSR.30); (CSR.33)--(CSR.34) merely separate it into convenient consequences.

---

## 7. The resultant jump is carried by at most `k` actual diameter vectors

Subtract the two exact sparse resultant equations (CSR.19):

\[
\begin{aligned}
F_{i+1}-F_i
&=\sum_h\widetilde\beta_h
 (\varepsilon_{h,i+1}-\varepsilon_{hi})r_h\\
&=\sum_{h\in J_i}\widetilde\beta_h
 (\varepsilon_{h,i+1}-\varepsilon_{hi})r_h,
\end{aligned}
\tag{CSR.35}
\]

where

\[
J_i
:=\{h\in\operatorname{supp}\widetilde\beta:
\varepsilon_{h,i+1}\ne\varepsilon_{hi}\}.
\]

At the selected boundary,

\[
\boxed{|J_i|\le k.}
\tag{CSR.36}
\]

Moreover

\[
\sum_{h\in J_i}\widetilde\beta_h
=\widetilde B_i
<{C_k^{\rm safe}\over D}+o(D^{-1}),
\tag{CSR.37}
\]

and therefore, since `|r_h|=D`,

\[
\boxed{
\|F_{i+1}-F_i\|
<C_k^{\rm safe}+o(1).
}
\tag{CSR.38}
\]

For `k=4`, equations (CSR.34)--(CSR.38) give precisely (CSR.1)--(CSR.3).

The adjacent switching theorem identifies every row in `J_i` as an edge of the layer
cut

\[
\delta_H(S_{i+1}\setminus S_i).
\]

That full layer-cut graph is a straight-line-thrackle caterpillar forest.  Hence the
sparse switching support `J_i` is a subgraph of that same one-dimensional switching
geometry.  In the four-row case the entire exact resultant jump is therefore encoded
by at most four edges of a diameter-thrackle layer cut.

---

## 8. Why this is different from the existing `N<1800` theorem

The Pareto adjacent-cut theorem bounds the **actual** switching set:

\[
N_i<1800+o(1)
\]

while retaining small original KKT switching mass and phase gap.  That statement is
about all active diameter constraints and is indispensable for the finite-cusp
analysis.

The present theorem does something different.  It compresses the vector of all
selected-cut KKT resultants to a sparse nonnegative certificate.  At one adjacent
boundary the difference of those exact resultants is then supported on at most four
actual diameter vectors.

Neither theorem implies the other:

- `N_i<1800` does not give a four-vector exact representation of `F_{i+1}-F_i`;
- the four-vector sparse certificate does not say the other actual switching diameter
  constraints disappear.

Together they give two complementary finite interfaces:

```text
actual active cusp:
    <1800 switching diameter edges, caterpillar forest, original beta mass O(1/D)

exact resultant certificate:
    <=4 switching diameter edges, same layer-cut geometry,
    exact F_i and F_{i+1}, sparse mass O(1/D), phase gap O(1/D)
```

The latter is a plausible finite classification target: any downstream argument that
uses only the two adjacent Section-157 cut resultants may now work with at most four
switching diameter vectors instead of an arbitrary bounded family.

---

## 9. Non-conclusions and next obligation

This theorem does **not** close C-II.

In particular:

1. `tilde beta` is not asserted to be a full KKT stress.  Full vertex equilibrium is
   not preserved by the Carathéodory projection used here.
2. Actual diameter constraints outside the sparse support remain active constraints of
   the source and cannot be ignored in a deformation argument.
3. No individual KKT multiplier lower bound is used or recovered.
4. No first-order feasible nonzero train-shear direction is claimed; the audited mixed
   rigidity/full-support KKT obstruction remains valid.

The gain is an exact reduction of the **cut-resultant interface**.  The next useful
step is to classify the possible four-edge layer-cut representations in (CSR.35)
together with

\[
F_i=A_i u_i,
\qquad
F_{i+1}=A_{i+1}u_{i+1},
\qquad
u_i-\nu_{i+1}=O(D^{-1}),
\]

and the cyclic/straight-line-thrackle order of their diameter endpoints.  A
contradiction there would attack the finite cusp without reintroducing the rejected
response-conductance inequality.

**Disposition:** proved a new exact Carathéodory sparsification and a four-to-eight-row
Pareto profile for the live adjacent diameter-resultant interface. C-II and Level I
remain open.
